=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables/nftables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically applied to traffic ingressing on `eth2` (the `r6_r3` link connecting router3 to router6).

**Mechanism:** Router6 is the sole gateway between the core routing fabric (routers 0–3, 5) and the 172.18.51.x subnet (accessed via mswitch2 → switch10). An ACL injected into its FORWARD chain accepts only one very narrow case — TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 — then drops all TCP, all UDP, and all remaining protocols (including ICMP) that enter on eth2. Because every cross-network packet to 172.18.51.x must transit router6 via eth2, all such traffic is silently dropped. Drop counters confirm 38 ICMP and 75 UDP packets have already been discarded. Hosts within the 172.18.51.x subnet can still reach each other because that traffic is local to mswitch2 and never passes through router6's FORWARD chain. Router6's own pings succeed because host-originated traffic uses the OUTPUT chain, not FORWARD.

**Localization:** Router6, interface eth2 (`r6_r3` link, IP 10.50.1.2/24), FORWARD chain rules 2–4.

**Root cause:** An overly restrictive iptables/nftables FORWARD ACL on router6 drops virtually all transit traffic arriving from the router3 direction, isolating the entire 172.18.51.x (Location C) subnet from every external network.

**Fix:** Flush or delete the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain: `iptables -F FORWARD` (and the equivalent nftables rules) — or selectively delete only the DROP rules to preserve any legitimate policy while restoring full transit routing.
=== END ===